oblem "Which of the following random variables can be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We can use the discrete uniform distribution to model the outcome of a single event when the support $S$ is finite, and each $x\\in S$ is equally likely.\n\nWith that in mind, let's consider each of the given random variables.\n\n- Random variable “The temperature of a town on a certain day” cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because a temperature is a real number, so the support is not finite.\n\n- Random variable “The number of letters of a word randomly selected from an English dictionary” cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. Each outcome is not equally likely (there are more short words than very long words, for example).\n\n- Random variable “The number of typos in a randomly selected book” cannot be modeled as a discrete uniform distribution. This is because the number of typos can be any positive integer. Moreover, each outcome is not equally likely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\""} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186426532 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186192780 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e3fe3596-340e-5155-bed3-cf25f5843d70" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Whether Sets Represented Graphically Are Subspaces in Two-Dimensional Space"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98e36e60-7f4e-5471-9c79-3de0fa9fb055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474254} {:answer/id #uuid "ecd5cedc-0fa5-539c-83af-8334b94a61b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474255}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "98e36e60-7f4e-5471-9c79-3de0fa9fb055" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 175